const PART: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "aq:1": [
    "At the South Pole station, the girl Lia saw a cracked stone on the science table. Inside was the print of a leaf, fine as lace. Lia frowned. ‘There is only ice here,’ she said. The scientist Mara smiled. ‘This leaf grew here, but the land was not at the end of the world yet.’ Antarctica did not always live at the pole. Fossils of forests, and even of dinosaurs, prove it. Before the tale, Mara asked for one thing. Everyone should hear the journey without fear, because the old map was green.",
    "Mara told the gentle legend, made to fit inside one station night. Antarctica was a stone boat, wide and slow, floating on a sea nobody sees, under the crust. Long ago it stood near warm lands, with tall trees and rivers. Dinosaurs walked in that forest, not knowing the ground would break. There was no cry and no fall. There was a slow push, millions of years long, like a boat leaving the quay without hurry. Lia imagined sails of cloud and a rudder of mountain. The stone on the table still seemed warm.",
    "The stone boat slid south, so slowly that no tree felt the step. The Sun, which once warmed the leaves, grew low and pale. The rivers learned to sleep. Science calls this continental drift, meaning the continents move over the Earth. The tale calls it a journey. Both say the same thing, with different words. Mara pointed at a map and moved her finger from the equator to the bottom. Lia saw the leaf moving away from the heat. The South Pole, in this story, was not waiting. The land came to it.",
    "When it reached the south, the stone boat was given a new dress. First came frost. Then came snow that did not melt in the short summer. At last came an ice sheet, white and thick, covering the forest like a sheet. The dinosaurs no longer walked there when the dress was complete. They had lived in the green time, and the stone kept their memory. Lia put her hand on the cold table and thought of roots under her feet. The ice did not erase the leaf. It only hid it, for someone who knows how to look.",
    "Lia went out to the ice with the leaf in her memory, because the fossil stays at the station. The sky was clear, and the ground seemed never to have been anything else. She knew that was a lie worth undoing. The South Pole was born like this, an ice dress put on land that was once green. The child understands that the map is not eternal. The continents still move, far too slowly for one day. Antarctica remembers the forest under the ice cap. Mara, at the door, said: ‘You are treading a journey.’ Lia nodded, and the white went quiet.",
  ],
  "aq:2": [
    "On the Antarctic Peninsula, the winter night lasts so long that the clock seems a joke. No ancient people lived on this ice, and the legend does not pretend otherwise. It was born in the huts of those who overwintered, scientists and explorers with worn-out spirits. Young Eva was on door duty, with her coat on and her tea already cold. Outside, the wind scraped the roof. Inside, the yellow light tired her eyes. Eva thought, very quietly: ‘If the dark goes on, I will forget the colour of day.’ Then the sky decided to answer.",
    "Eva opened the door and saw curtains of green and red moving. It was the southern lights, the aurora australis, sister of the northern lights. Explorers gave it names of cold fire. It shines like a flame and does not warm your face. The light asked no permission from the hut or from Eva's shift. It danced over the ice, rose, fell and rose again, as if the sky knew a game. Eva forgot her tea. Her spirit, about to break, stopped for a moment to look. The cold fire did not burn. It showed that the dark did not own everything.",
    "On the shelf was an old hut notebook, written in crooked letters by someone in gloves. Eva read a page by the light of the open door. Some winterers wrote that the aurora was the wave of the dead of the expeditions. It did not come to frighten. It came to say that the path existed, even when the ship did not return. Eva did not laugh at the sentence and was not afraid. She closed her coat and looked up again. The colours moved like slow hands, with no hurry. In the notebook, someone had added: ‘If the spirit leaves, the light arrives.’",
    "Eva stayed at the door until her hands hurt with cold. Her chest, though, was quieter. The aurora promised no boat and no summer there. It promised company. Next day the wind was still hard and the tea went cold again. The work went on: measuring the ice, writing numbers, keeping the door. The difference was small and it was enough. When her spirit threatened to leave again, Eva looked at the window. Sometimes the sky was only dark. Sometimes the cold fire returned without warning. She told her colleagues: ‘The light comes when it is needed, even if nobody calls it.’",
    "This tale is folklore of a science hut, recent and honest, not a myth of a thousand years. The aurora does not ask permission to dance, and the ice below seems to listen. A child looking at photographs at home can hear that the southern sky plays too. Eva left a new line in the notebook: ‘I saw the cold fire, and the path still exists.’ Those who overwinter next will read it and perhaps smile in the dark. The light does not erase winter. It arrives when the spirit leaves, and in Antarctica that is already a hand held out.",
  ],
  "aq:3": [
    "In 1914, Captain Ernest Shackleton entered the Weddell Sea with the ship Endurance and a whole crew. They wanted to cross Antarctica. The ice had another plan. The plates closed around the hull and the ship was stuck, still, like a seed inside a hard fruit. There was no harbour and no road. Shackleton looked at the men, one by one, and said the sentence the legend kept: ‘We all go.’ It was not a king's speech. It was a simple promise. The south, that year, was going to test the word.",
    "The months passed and the ice pressed harder, until the Endurance cracked and was swallowed. The ship disappeared under the plates. The men stayed on top, with tents, lifeboats and the same promise. The gentle version asks for no wounds and no shouting. It asks for the cold, the waiting and a captain who refused to leave anyone. Shackleton shared the little there was and slept like the others. One night he said, for the wind to hear fully: ‘The ice ate the ship. It does not eat my word.’ When the hour to leave came, nobody was abandoned on the plate. The word was still whole.",
    "When the ice opened a channel of water, they put the boats to sea. The waves were high and the cold soaked their hands, but the tale needs no more fright than this. They reached a stony island, and part of the men waited there. They had counted food and the order to hold on together. Shackleton chose a small boat and a few companions. He pointed at South Georgia, on the other side of a rough sea. He was not going for glory. He was going for help for those who had stayed. In the boat he repeated, softly: ‘We all go.’",
    "On South Georgia, the captain and his companions crossed mountains of ice and stone. They had no sure map, until they found people and a rescue ship. Then Shackleton came back by sea and went for those waiting on the island. One by one, they climbed onto the deck. He did not lose a single man. There was no dragon and no fairy opening the way. There was a stubborn man keeping his word, and others trusting it when their bodies asked for rest. The ice had eaten the Endurance. It had not eaten ‘we all go’. Children understand this hero without needing a crown.",
    "Antarctica keeps this tale as other continents keep kings. For a long time the Weddell Sea hid the ship's wood, in the dark depths. Now they have found it, quiet, under the water. The legend had arrived first, from mouth to mouth, without waiting for the find. Shackleton remains, in the gentle version, the captain who refused to leave anyone. If you see a small boat on an enormous map, remember the sentence. Going all together is not going fast. It is not letting anyone go on the ice, even when the boat no longer exists.",
  ],
  "aq:4": [
    "In the Antarctic summer, the sea opens dark patches between the ice. It fills with tiny shrimps, which scientists call krill. There are no human kings at the pole. There are guests. The grandmother humpback whale arrives with her granddaughter Lila, after thousands of kilometres. She does not come to take anyone's home. She comes to the krill table, to eat, rest and sing. Lila swims in her grandmother's shadow and learns the cold smell of the water. For them, this ice is a summer door, not a throne.",
    "The grandmother sings underwater, a phrase the boats can hear with instruments. Scientists record those songs and notice something amazing. They change from year to year, like fashions, and the other humpbacks learn the new version. The gentle legend says the whale carries messages between the ice and the warmth. One year the song speaks of currents. Another year it speaks of a safer path. Lila repeats the notes, still crooked, and her grandmother waits. The whole ocean is their village. There is no wall between the pole and the tropics, only water and memory.",
    "Before setting off north, the grandmother shows Lila that this journey does not begin in Antarctica. The peoples of Polynesia and South America already had the humpback as an ancestor, before the maps. On the warm islands it is old family. On the southern ice it is a summer guest. Both things are true at the same time. Lila hears the song and imagines palm trees, though she knows only icebergs. The grandmother owns neither place. She is the grandmother of fins who links the two tables. One is krill, the other the warm waters where calves are born.",
    "The route is long. They leave the ice, cross wide seas and reach the warmth, where the song is heard better. Lila learns the turns of the path: a mountain of water here, a coast there, a place to rest. There is no war and no escape in this tale. There is a grandmother teaching the music of the sea, note by note. On the way they meet other humpbacks and swap the new fashion of the song. It is like people swapping news in the square. Lila no longer sings out of tune. When the water warms, she understands the message: ice and warmth speak through them.",
    "Next summer, the grandmother returns to Antarctica, and Lila goes with her, bigger and surer. The krill table is still set. They eat, sing that year's song and do not stay for ever. To tell of the humpback on this continent is to tell of a guest, not an owner. The pole has no human kings. It has this grandmother of fins who returns, who sings and who leaves. A child who hears the recording learns the route without leaving home. The sea is the music. Each verse changes, and still the ice door always opens for those who know the way.",
  ],
  "za:0": [
    "How the zebra got its stripes is a San and Khoekhoe tale, told by the fire in South Africa. At the beginning the zebra was white, or the colour of sand. It drank at the waterholes of the dry land. It was seen from far off, in the pale grass. In these stories, the animals were not born ready. The world painted them, at the cost of thirst, of arguments and of chances that last for ever. By the San and Khoekhoe fire nobody is in a hurry. A painting of the world is not told in a rush.",
    "One day the zebra argued with a baboon beside a waterhole. The baboon did not want to share the water, and the two shouted until the dust rose. In the confusion, the zebra rolled too close to the baboon's fire. This is not a story for laughing at a wound. It is the story of a quarrel that reaches the fire because nobody gave up a place on the bank. The waterhole, which should be shared, became a stage, as happens when thirst mixes with pride.",
    "The embers painted its body black, in stripes, from mane to hooves. It got up already striped for ever. Another version, told by the same peoples, says the quarrel was with a bat. The zebra rolled in the fire after that fight. The end is the same. The fire writes on the coat, and the waterhole stays behind with the lesson still hot. Some swear it was the bat, not the baboon, but the stripes on the coat tell of the same afternoon.",
    "The zebra became hard to see in the shade of the trees. It also vanished in the tall grass, where light splits the colours. It drinks quickly and runs away, beautiful because it is so warned. The baboon also learned something about the waterhole: shared water avoids a spoiled afternoon. Nobody goes back to the colour of sand. The new design is the price and, at the same time, the protection. In the tall grass the zebra half vanishes, which is a strange gift born of an old fight.",
    "South Africa tells this by the fire, with the children close and the sky big. The animals did not come finished from the first morning: the world painted them. If you see a zebra, you can remember the waterhole, the baboon and the embers. Some speak of the bat instead of the baboon, and both voices fit. The stripe is the memory of a thirst that ended in fire. If you see the stripes in the sun, remember that even beauty can begin in a fight over water.",
  ],
  "za:1": [
    "In the Zulu and Xhosa folklore of South Africa, the tokoloshe is a small spirit of the night. Sometimes people speak of him as a fright for the child who will not sleep. Sometimes as a problem called up by someone who wishes others harm. The elders take him seriously, because he is not a film clown and not a party mask. It is a story that asks for a low voice. In Zulu and Xhosa homes the name is said quietly. It is not for play, but so that the night is taken seriously.",
    "The gentle version, the one told to a child, is this. The tokoloshe punishes those who treat others badly and frightens those who refuse sleep. He does not need to be seen to be remembered. He is small, and the rule he brings is big. He does not hunt innocent people to make a show. He follows the offence, like a message the night sends back. In this gentle version the fright is not a monster on the loose. It is the offence knocking at the door again.",
    "That is why, in many homes, beds are put on bricks. He can pass underneath and not disturb those who sleep. A Xhosa grandmother shows the bricks to her grandson. She says the night has its ways. It is not a building game, but respect for the invisible. The high bed leaves a free space, and that space says: we know the rule and we want no trouble. The bricks under the bed are a visible custom of a belief you do not see with your eyes.",
    "People tell of a boy who mocked a neighbour and hid something that was not his. At night he heard a rustling beside his bed and did not sleep a wink. In the morning he said sorry and gave back what he had taken. The next night was quiet, and the story promises no circus magic. It promises that words and deeds come back, sometimes when the house has already put out the light. The boy learned that giving back what is not ours calms a house faster than any shouting.",
    "City life in South Africa still lifts the bed, in Johannesburg and other cities. The legend did not stay only in the village. It asks for respect for the invisible and for a neighbour's word. If you treat someone badly and think nobody saw, the tale says otherwise. Some offences come back at night, and the creature is small, and the rule does not fit under the bed. In Johannesburg the high bed still says the same sentence: respect did not stay only in the old village.",
  ],
  "za:2": [
    "The Rain Queen, Modjadji, of the Lovedu people in Limpopo, is true history and legend at the same time. A family of queens, it is said, could call the rain when the land cracked. The power passed from woman to daughter, in the north of South Africa. It was not a tale loose in the air. It had a village, a name and a duty, year after year. In Limpopo the name Modjadji is not a story decoration, but a duty passed from mother to daughter.",
    "When the drought pressed hard, the people went to ask, with respect, not with orders. They walked to the queen's place and spoke of thirst, of seeds that would not grow and of tired animals. Modjadji listened, and science would later measure the clouds. The people, at that time, measured the queen. The two ways of looking at the sky met on the same throne. Neither of them needed to insult the other. Asking for rain there was walking with respect, not stamping at the sky as if it were a servant.",
    "In a very dry year, messengers came from far villages in Limpopo, with dust on their sandals. The clouds were thin, and the queen received them under the trees. She did not turn the request into a show. The story says the rain answered the kingdom, not a shout. Waiting was also part of it. Whoever asks for water learns that the sky is not a servant, and the queen is not one either. The messengers waited under the trees, because hurrying fills no well and persuades no cloud.",
    "The line followed from mother to daughter, and each queen inherited the duty together with the name. It was not enough to sit in a high place. You had to care for the relation between the people and the rain. If the answer was late, the waiting was a responsibility, not an excuse. In this tale, the throne is not for shining. It is for someone to answer when the land is thirsty. Each daughter who inherited the place also inherited the thirst of others, which is a real queen's weight.",
    "To tell of Modjadji is to honour a feminine power rare in the folklore of the world. The north of South Africa still knows the name. The rain, in this story, has a queen, and the queen has a duty. If you see the sky closing over a dry field, the old way is to ask with respect. Clouds have their causes, and the Lovedu people also keep the memory of someone who could listen. You can measure the clouds and remember that there were queens in charge of hearing the land.",
  ],
  "za:3": [
    "In Zulu myth, Unkulunkulu is the first ancestor, the beginning of people. He came out of the reeds, where water and green meet, and brought people and cattle into the world. The story also speaks of death, but it speaks seriously, not with fright on purpose. It is a myth of origin. The version for children fits in two animals, two messages and a hurry that changes fate. The reeds from which Unkulunkulu came are still, for the Zulu people, the old door of the world.",
    "Unkulunkulu sent a chameleon with good news: humans would not die. The chameleon set off as chameleons do, slowly, stopping, changing colour, looking at every branch. It was not wicked laziness, it was his way. The message of a life without end went in his short step. The road was longer than it had looked in the morning. The chameleon was not bad, he was slow, and that difference matters when the message is about life.",
    "A lizard took the opposite message and ran faster and arrived first. The word that came too early was the hard word, and so people do not live for ever. When the chameleon arrived at last, the first news was already in the world. He was not cruel, he was late. The reeds, the cattle and the people remained, and only the promise of never ending missed its hour. The lizard, by arriving first, changed the message of the world without needing to be bigger than a hand.",
    "Zulu children hear this as great things are heard: slowly. Two messages, two animals, and the hurry changing the fate of the world. Nobody asks you to fear the lizard or to scorn the chameleon. They ask for attention. An important word should not be left for later, if the road is long. Here the delay is not a joke, but the explanation of a limit. Children learn here that a late word may find the house already arranged differently.",
    "The Zulu South Africa keeps Unkulunkulu as the first. The legend does not hide death: it explains it with a delay. The slow chameleon still today seems the excuse of the eternity that did not arrive. If you see him on a branch, you can remember the reeds and the message that was on its way. Some words cannot wait, and others, when they arrive late, find the world already changed. If you see a chameleon still on a branch, do not laugh at his slowness. Wait until you know what message he brings.",
  ],
  "za:4": [
    "In the Nguni tales of South Africa, the hare and the lion are a famous pair of the savannah. The lion is the king and roars, and the hare is small, hungry and thinking. She is a sister of Sungura, the hare of East Africa, and of the clever rabbit of Angolan tales. Many languages share this duo. The village laughs at the king, not out of malice, but because strength without brains loses. The Nguni savannah laughs warmly at the king, because laughing at a distracted strong one also teaches.",
    "On a hot day, the waterhole showed a face. The lion believed another lion was stealing his water. He roared at his own reflection, splashing and angry with the image. While he argued with the water, the hare drank freely and moved away in silence. The king was strong, not attentive. The quiet waterhole had only given him back his own face. The reflection at the waterhole stole nothing. It only gave the lion back the angry face he had brought himself.",
    "Later the hare shouted: ‘The sky is going to fall!’ The lion, big and quick to be frightened, bent down and covered his head. The hare went past, light, and did not stay to explain the joke. When he understood the trick, there was only grass and wind. The roar came late. On the savannah, whoever thinks a second earlier reaches the shade first, even with short paws. The sky, of course, did not fall, but the lion bent down anyway. That is the power of a sentence said in time.",
    "There was also the sharing of the hunt. The lion wanted everything and called that a king's justice. The hare divided the portions with clever words. The best piece stayed with her, and the lion, confused, accepted. Hunger that thinks can move a pile that strength only guards. It was not a fight of claws, but a conversation in which the king did not count to the end. The unfair share turned around without claws, with words alone, until the best piece quietly changed owner.",
    "When the lion finally understood, he roared, and the hare had already gone. She does not sleep two nights in the same place. The village laughs, and the message is old: strength that does not think loses to hunger that thinks. If you are the small person in the story, there is still a way. It is not the roar. It is attention, the waterhole, the pretend sky and leaving in time. The hare was already far away, somewhere else. Whoever fools a king does not sleep twice in the same grass bed.",
  ],
  "ng:0": [
    "Anansi, the clever spider of the Akan stories, is also told in Nigeria. People tell it in the shade of the trees, beside Ijapa and other tricksters. One day Anansi decided that all the wisdom should be his alone. Then nobody else would know as much as he did. He went from village to village and heard the elders, the hunters and the mothers. He put each piece of advice into a calabash. When it was heavy and full, he laughed: ‘Now the knowledge is mine, and you, world, go without it.’",
    "Anansi wanted to hide the calabash at the top of a tall tree. No person and no animal could reach it there. He tied it to his belly, in front, and began to climb. But the calabash banged against the trunk and his knees could not reach the bark. He tried again, and again, angry and sweating. All the wisdom in the world was blocking his way. Below, his son Ntikuma watched everything and did not laugh out loud. He still respected his father, even when his father was wrong.",
    "Ntikuma spoke carefully: ‘Father, put the calabash on your back. Your belly is free and the tree lets you climb.’ Anansi stopped, looked at his son and felt a warm shame. The idea had not come from the calabash. It had come from the ground. He understood then that wisdom does not fit in one man, or in one spider. The calabash cannot hold it. If the son, without the calabash, knew something the father did not, the plan had failed.",
    "Anansi, angry at his own pride, threw the calabash to the ground. It broke against the roots. Wisdom spread everywhere: one piece of advice for the grass, another for the river, another for the village people. Nobody could gather it whole again. Since that day knowledge has gone about the world broken, a little in each head. Anansi came down from the tree lighter, and Ntikuma gave him his hand. He did not say ‘I am the one who knows’, because the lesson was already on the ground.",
    "In Nigeria, among Yoruba, Igbo and Hausa people, there are other clever ones, like the tortoise Ijapa. This Anansi tale keeps walking, though. It teaches the sharing of knowledge. If you keep everything for yourself, you cannot even climb the tree where you wanted to hide it. The broken calabash is the school of the world, because each person kept a piece and nobody owned the rest. When you learn something good, do not tie it to your belly. Pass it on, because the ground sometimes sees better than the top.",
  ],
  "ng:1": [
    "Ijapa, the tortoise of Yoruba stories, lives in the south-west of Nigeria. She is greedy, slow of paw and very fast of idea. People laugh at her like a clever uncle who is too clever. He reaches the feast before the others and still asks for pudding. Her shell, painted with old designs, looks like the notebook where each trick was written. That way the village would not forget. In Yoruba land, between Oyo and Ibadan, children know her name at once.",
    "One day Ijapa had a greedy idea. She asked all her in-laws, from several villages, to give her a feast on the same day. She wanted to eat in every house and taste every pot. She also wanted leftovers on her shell, without missing one invitation. She told each family: ‘Cook at midday, I will be there on time.’ She forgot that one body does not split into four. Greed did the sums, and the sums fitted in her head. They did not fit the dirt road between the villages.",
    "At midday Ijapa set off running, which for a tortoise is almost flying. Hunger pulled her two ways. Halfway between one in-law's house and another's, the sun pressed hard. Her paws stopped obeying. She stayed stuck there, smelling the feasts far away and reaching none. The pots grew cold without her. When she finally reached the first house, there were only empty pots and laughter. Someone said: ‘You wanted everything, and you got nothing.’",
    "In another tale, Ijapa wanted fame as a strong one. She went to the elephant and the hippo, each in their own place. She told the elephant: ‘You cannot pull a rope against me.’ Far from the other, she told the hippo the same. She tied the rope between the two and hid in the middle. She let the giants pull, each sure they were fighting the tortoise. They tired against each other, the ground shook, and Ijapa appeared fresh, as if she had beaten both.",
    "Ijapa became famous in the village. For some days she walked with her neck high, telling about the rope as if it were a real deed. At other times the tale punishes her. Greedy cleverness, the feast kind, does not end in a party. It ends in the middle of the road. The lesson changes. Sometimes it warns against greed, sometimes it shows useful cleverness. That kind solves a problem without being the strongest. The slow tortoise always reaches tricks first. If you are clever, do not book two lunches at the same hour.",
  ],
  "ng:2": [
    "In Ife, the sacred city of the Yoruba in Nigeria, the sky was once very close. People say it was so close you could almost touch it with your hand. The clouds rested on the roofs. Children ran under the blue as if under a cloth, and the divine was a neighbour. People, hungry or in a hurry, cut pieces of sky to eat. They did it as one cuts yam, without asking and without thanking. The sky hurt, but it still stayed there. It waited for someone to speak quietly to what is above.",
    "The last insult came from the women's pestles. They went up and down in the mortar. They went so high that they hit the sky with every stroke. It was not the cruelty of one person. It was the habit of treating the high as the kitchen ceiling, useful and without a name. Olodumare, the greater god, grew tired of the lack of respect. Then came one more stroke, the stroke that would no longer be forgiven. There was no ugly punishing thunder. The sky simply began to move away.",
    "People stretched their arms and could not reach it. Then they stood on benches, and the benches were too short too. The sky rose until it was blue and high. It was lovely to see and impossible to cut. The yam above stopped being food. A woman put down her pestle, looked up and told her son: ‘You no longer touch the sky. Now you can only call it.’ The boy tried on tiptoe. He laughed awkwardly, under a neighbour who had moved very far away.",
    "Since then people pray for Olodumare to hear. The voice has to climb a long road now, because the sky is no longer beside us. In Ife they still tell this to children when they bang a stick on the roof. The story asks for respect for what is above. Not paralysed fear, but the care not to use up what covers us. Hunger, hurry and the pestle moved the divine away. Now it hears, if it hears at all, only when someone calls with care.",
    "The Yoruba Nigeria still speaks of a sky that was once a neighbour. It was so close that food and cloud almost blended. Today the blue is high and the birds make the middle of the way. People stay on the ground, learning the measure. If you hit what is above you, even if it seems only a ceiling, remember the pestle. What cannot be touched can still be called. Olodumare, up there, still listens to those who speak with respect.",
  ],
  "ng:3": [
    "On the savannah of Nigeria, under an enormous baobab, a lion was napping. The harmattan wind was bringing fine dust from the north. A small savannah mouse, with bright eyes, ran over his paw by accident, escaping a shadow. The lion woke and put his paw on the mouse. He opened his mouth, already thinking it was just a moment of food. The mouse spoke quickly: ‘Spare me, king of the savannah. A small creature can also repay a big favour.’",
    "The lion laughed, a deep laugh that shook the grass. He found it funny that a mouse was making speeches under his paw. He lifted his paw and said: ‘Go, I am not hungry for such little things. You would not even be a toothful.’ The mouse did not run off without thanking him. He looked the lion in the eyes and promised to remember, though the strong one was already forgetting. That is how it is in this fable, told with baobabs and harmattan dust. The strong forgets, the weak does not.",
    "Some time later, a hunter from the village set a sisal net in the grass. He placed it where the lion used to drink. The lion fell into the net and struggled. The more he pulled, the tighter the sisal grew, until his mane was full of knots. He roared, and the roar crossed the savannah. The other animals, though, were afraid of the hunter and did not come near. The mouse heard it, knew the voice and ran to the baobab. The debt of mercy was still unpaid.",
    "The mouse came to the net, small among the big knots. He said: ‘Be still, king, I have teeth to fit this.’ He gnawed the sisal with patience, thread by thread. The lion breathed deeply and learned not to move against someone helping him. The net opened and the lion came out. The hunter, far away, found only a hole and a trail that was not coming back. The lion lowered his head, enormous before the mouse. He said: ‘You have paid, and I will not laugh at a small favour again.’",
    "The children of the village understand the pair easily. The lion could do anything except get out of a sisal net alone. The mouse did not forget the day he was spared. That memory was worth more than claws, mane and roar together. The fable of Aesop became Nigerian when the hunter is from the village. The net is of sisal and the harmattan dust is in the air. A favour, even to a mouse, is a knot. One day it comes undone in your favour, if you know how to spare the small.",
  ],
  "ng:4": [
    "The River Niger crosses Nigeria like a long goddess. It is wide at Onitsha, full of fish, boats and old rules. On the bank lived a girl who washed the fish with care. She never dirtied the water just because it was easier. The elders spoke of Mami Wata, the water mother. She has long hair, watches the river and sometimes leaves gifts in the sand. It was not a gift to show off in the village. It was a secret, and whoever did not keep it lost the way home.",
    "One afternoon the girl found a comb and a mirror on the bank. They shone, and the sun was not enough to explain that shine. Mami Wata's voice rose from the water: ‘They are yours if you respect the river. But if you show them, the current takes the way.’ The girl did not scream or call her friends. She wrapped the comb and the mirror in a cloth and took them home in silence. That night the family ate fish and the pot was enough for everyone. The river seemed to thank her for the old care.",
    "A neighbour saw the cloth and asked: ‘Show me what the river gave you. I want a comb like that on my mat too.’ The girl remembered the voice and shook her head: ‘I cannot. You would have to ask the Niger, not me.’ In another village, people told of a girl who showed the gift laughing. The river called her back until she disappeared from the bank. It was not a punishment of shouts. The rule was clear: the secret of the water stays off the mat.",
    "The girl kept the secret for many days. She washed the fish with gratitude and poured the dirty water away from the bank where people drank. The family prospered without a noisy party. There was fish in the pot and the boat did not tear. The net came back full in the right measure. Mami Wata, long hair mixing with the current, watched without coming up to the village. The agreement was being kept. At night the girl combed her hair with the hidden comb. The mirror's light stayed only on the river.",
    "The peoples of the Niger bank know these rules. They do not tell them with an easy smile, because the water gives and takes. The girl of the legend is the whole people. She accepts the gift, keeps quiet and thanks the fish and the river the same way. If you go to Onitsha, or to any wide bend of the Niger, do not throw rubbish in the water. It is not ownerless. Mami Wata still watches, with long hair. A well-kept secret still brings the pot round at the end of the day.",
  ],
  "ma:0": [
    "In Morocco, beside the palm trees and the springs, people speak of Aicha Kandicha. She is a jinniya, a water spirit, and tradition treats her name with care, not like a fairground mascot. It is said she has strange eyes, like a goat's. She appears when someone walks alone at night, in a place that is not theirs. Mothers call that name to gather the children before dark. The gnawa sing of her. The springs and the hammams have rules, hours and silences that are not broken for fun.",
    "In a village of palm trees, the boy Karim thought the rule was only talk for tired people. He stayed out after dark, alone, beside a spring that served the whole neighbourhood. He wanted to prove he was not afraid of a name. The water was still. The palm trees made a dark corridor. Karim laughed out loud and said: ‘If Aicha exists, let her come and see me. I will not run.’ Nobody answered. Only the spring, repeating his laugh in a colder voice. He took one more step, past the stone where mothers tell you to stop.",
    "The path between the palms began to look the same every time. Karim turned left and found the spring again. In the water he saw two strange eyes, still, that were not his. No hand grabbed him and there was no film scream. The place itself closed in, as if the night were saying: you do not live here. He remembered, late, that you do not mock a name beside the water. He sat on the stone, without running blindly, and asked permission in a low voice. The fear stayed, but the mocking had ended.",
    "At home, Karim's mother lit an oil lamp and went to the edge of the palm grove. She did not enter the spring area. She called him by his own name, not by the spirit's name. Then she repeated the sentence the elders use to bring someone back: ‘The water stays, and you come back.’ Karim heard the right voice and followed it, his back to the reflection. The lamp appeared between two trunks. He reached his mother with wet feet and no story to boast about. The spring stayed behind, keeping its hour, without following him to bed.",
    "The next day, Karim did not go back alone to the spring at night. He did not laugh at the name either. He learned the rules of the hammam and of shared water. You go in at the right hours. You do not make it dirty. You do not stay when the place has closed. Morocco does not turn Aicha Kandicha into a doll. The legend asks for modesty and the right hour. In the Maghreb, the jinn joins the conversation like a neighbour, with respect. Aicha is the most spoken of. If you hear her name, respect the water, the night and the word.",
  ],
  "ma:1": [
    "In the medinas of Morocco, the old palaces and the tales of the nights mix easily. There was a sultan who hid a treasure in a chest, at the back of a courtyard. He did not want to leave it to the vizier. The vizier was clever with papers and poor in justice. Every time he passed the door, his eyes went straight to the lock. The sultan saw and said nothing. He told only the servants: ‘The key is not for whoever wants it loudest. It is for the hand that gives back what is not its own.’",
    "In the souk, the boy Amin found a silver coin in the dust, beside the bread stall. He could keep it. Nobody was looking. He wiped it on his sleeve and asked, stall by stall, until he found the woman. She blessed him without any fuss. The sultan was walking in disguise among the baskets and saw the whole gesture. That afternoon he called Amin to the courtyard. He put the key in his hand and said: ‘The vizier wanted this. You gave back a coin. Now you guard the chest.’",
    "At night Amin opened the chest, his heart beating, and saw gold enough for a life of silk. He thought of his mother, of the crooked roof and of the quarter where bread ran short on Fridays. He did not buy a party suit. He bought flour, oil and bread. He had them shared at the doors of the medina, starting with the silent houses. The chest, says the legend, did not run out. In the morning there were still coins, just the ones needed for the next day, not one more for vanity. The gold seemed to listen to its use.",
    "The vizier could not bear it. He entered the courtyard while Amin slept, took the key from under the mat and filled his pockets. He wanted the centre of the treasure, not the shared edge. The coins in his hand lost their shine and became like rust, light and useless. The chest closed with a click and did not open again for that man. In the morning the sultan found the vizier with dirty pockets and not one sentence to save his greed. Amin, awake, did not shout. He only said: ‘The key is still in the hand that breaks the bread.’",
    "Fez, Marrakesh and Meknes each tell their own variant, with another courtyard and the same core. The essential does not change: the key stays in the right hand, and gold without sharing rusts. The honest boy did not become rich for show. He became the keeper of a chest that lasts only while it feeds the quarter. The child of the medina understands this quickly, because they see the bread passing from door to door. If you receive a key, first ask who is hungry beside you. The sultan's treasure does not run out in the hand that knows how to divide.",
  ],
  "ma:2": [
    "In the Middle Atlas, where snow covers the paths in winter, Amazigh weavers make carpets that are more than floor. Every diamond, every colour, remembers a tribe, a pasture, a spring. Amina taught her daughter Imane beside the loom and always left a small mistake. ‘Only God is perfect,’ she said. ‘That mistake is the door. Without it, the wool grows proud and heavy.’ Imane thought the sentence lovely and a little strange. She wanted a carpet without flaw, worthy of flying like in the old stories.",
    "Imane wove for whole nights a carpet with no mistake at all: right thread, right colour, right pride. When she finished, she put her hands on it and asked: ‘Fly over the Atlas.’ The carpet did not move. It stayed on the floor, beautiful and deaf. That week the snow closed the mountain pass. On the other side, Imane's grandmother was in a village without a road. She needed a blanket and a bag of bread, not a speech. The men said nobody would pass until spring. The perfect carpet felt heavier than the snow.",
    "Amina did not scold her. She sat beside her daughter and pointed at a knot in the centre. ‘Undo this one, on purpose, and do not boast about the design.’ Imane held back. The carpet seemed the best thing her hands had made. Then she thought of her grandmother, of the cold and of the sentence about the door. She undid one knot only. She asked again, with no audience and no vanity. ‘If I go, I take the blanket and the bread, and I come back.’ The wool trembled, like an animal waking. The small mistake had opened what perfection closed.",
    "The carpet rose slowly, to the height of a roof. Then it rose higher, until the Middle Atlas was below like a white map. Imane did not shout. She held the bag and the blanket and let the wool choose the wind. They passed peaks, tiny villages and a black river between the snow. It was not an escape for ever. It was a delivery. They landed in her grandmother's courtyard. The grandmother covered her granddaughter with both hands and asked no long questions. The bread was broken. The blanket covered the shoulders.",
    "They came back before midday, and the carpet landed beside the loom. It was floor of the house once more. Imane did not close the mistake. She left it, as her mother had taught, because the door is not sewn up after use. In Morocco this flight is told as a wish, and the sacred mistake is told as a law. Amazigh carpets are still maps of a tribe, read by those who know. The child who watches the weaver understands the legend in her hands. The snowy Atlas already seems made to be flown over by wool, when the pattern is not perfect.",
  ],
  "ma:3": [
    "Fez is a city of narrow streets, of mosques and of courtyards where water sings quietly. In the tanneries, hides dry in the sun. Above the houses walk pigeons, many of them, as if the medina had another map in the air. At the Al Quaraouiyine University, already old when others had not been born, students bend over their books. One of those pigeons, says the legend, is not only looking for crumbs. It knows the springs, the Andalusian courtyards, and the hour when a person should stop reading to drink.",
    "Yusuf had studied since dawn and had forgotten his water jar. The letters seemed more important than thirst. In the late afternoon he went out to buy bread. Without meaning to, he got caught in a tangle of identical alleys. A blue door, then another, a cat, a smell of leather, and he no longer knew where the school was. His throat hurt. He sat on a step and laughed awkwardly. ‘I know how to recite a page and I do not know how to get home,’ he said. Above, a white pigeon beat its wings and landed on a grille. It waited, like someone who knows the maze by heart.",
    "In Fez they tell that pigeons have already carried simple messages between courtyards. They carried a good sentence from one window to another. One of them revealed a hidden spring when the city was thirsty. Yusuf did not ask for a miracle of gold. He asked for path and water. The pigeon jumped two roofs and looked back. He followed it, slowly, without running, because in the medina hurry closes alleys. They passed an Andalusian courtyard, a wool shop and a corner he had seen three times from the wrong side. The pigeon was in no hurry. It knew the exit from above.",
    "They stopped at a small spring, half hidden by a lemon tree. The water came out clear and with no queue. The pigeon drank first, sideways, and Yusuf understood. It was not only the path that was wrong. It was the hour. He had studied until he dried up. He drank slowly, washed his hands and thanked her quietly, as if the spring were a teacher. The pigeon waited. Then it went up in a straight line, and he saw, between two minarets, the roof of the Al Quaraouiyine. He followed that line on foot, counting the doors, until the school jar appeared in its usual place.",
    "Yusuf began to put the jar beside the book, and to drink before the page pulled him far away. Knowledge, said the teacher, also gets thirsty. Fez is still a maze for those who look only at their feet. The pigeon, from above, knows the exit, and the legend gave the city a guide with wings. If you get lost among the alleys, stop and look at the sky. Sometimes help is not at the next corner. It is in the bird that has already drunk and is waiting for you. The lost child does the same. Sometimes it works.",
  ],
  "ma:4": [
    "On Fridays, in many Moroccan homes, couscous is more than food. It is a rite. The big pot goes on the fire and the semolina rises in steam. On top goes the tfaya, sweet onion with raisins and a smell that crosses the courtyard. In this Fez house lived a thin cat, a roof neighbour. He knew the day by his nose. As soon as the tfaya was ready, he jumped onto the wall. He kept looking at the centre of the dish, where the onion shone. The mistress, Lalla Fatima, saw him and did not pick up the broom.",
    "The cat tried his luck. He stretched out a paw towards the tfaya, the sweet part. Everybody keeps it for the middle of the table. Lalla Fatima covered the centre with an open hand, without hitting, and laughed. ‘A guest, even with four paws, has a right,’ she said. ‘But the right is the edge, not the heart of the dish.’ She put the edge of the couscous in a bowl for him, without sweet onion. He ate on the clean floor, beside the family. The cat ate slowly. For the first time he did not steal. He had been invited.",
    "That night a mouse climbed into the pantry, straight for the sack of grain. The cat, with a quiet belly and a marked place, did not make a fighting scene. He stood at the door, meowed once, firmly. The mouse turned back before touching the sack. In Moroccan homes people also say that the cat watches the corners where a snake might come in. That is why its place is old and respected. Here there was no ugly fright. There was a warning in time. Lalla Fatima lit the lamp, closed the gap and thanked the neighbour.",
    "In the courtyard next door, a man ate alone and chased away every cat. He treated the table like a secret. His couscous finished quickly, and nobody warned him when the pantry was left open. One afternoon he saw Lalla Fatima's cat sitting on the wall. It looked at him without asking out loud. He felt judged, not by a speech, but by those quiet eyes. He took a bowl to the edge of the wall. The cat accepted. Since then, when a mouse passed, there was someone to meow again. The house that breaks the grain stays at peace.",
    "The Friday couscous kept coming out of the pot. The tfaya stayed in the middle for the people, and the edge went to the guest. The cat did not put a paw in the big dish again. He knew his place, and the place was good. In Fez he is not a decoration. He is a neighbour. The gentle legend teaches hospitality, and Morocco is often measured by its table. The cat judges whoever eats alone. If you have little, leave the edge anyway. A small guest may be the one guarding the pantry while you sleep.",
  ],
  "mz:0": [
    "The Island of Mozambique is small, made of stone and coral. For a long time it was a port where Swahili, Persian and Portuguese boats crossed. On the quay you still see dhows, doors of carved wood and capulanas of bright colours. The island was once a capital, and the whole country took its name from it. Legends mix with this true history. People speak of a mermaid at the tip and of old treasures in Sancul. The heart of the tale, though, is the island itself, still in the shallow sea.",
    "The elders say the island was not an island at the beginning. It was an enormous stone fish, swimming slowly off Mossuril. One day it heard a song coming from the shallow water. It was a song without hurry, like someone calling another by their true name. The stone fish stopped to listen better. The waves beat on its sides, the coral took hold, and it forgot to leave. The song ended. The fish stayed. That is how the island was born, with an attentive ear.",
    "On top of that still fish grew houses, a chapel, a palace of captains. Alleys grew too, where salt dries on the walls. The children go down to the quay and see the legend in the coral. There are pale patches, urchin holes, a coast that looks like scales. When the sea of Mossuril is shallow and blue, it seems it said yes to the spell. The capital of the country moved elsewhere. The myth did not move. It stayed on the island, for those who arrive by boat and ask about the name.",
    "The mermaid at the tip, on other nights, sings so that boats do not get lost on the shallows. She does not ask them to follow her to the bottom. She asks them to look at the coast and respect the stones. The treasures of Sancul, if they ever existed in gold, have already been spread through time by true history. The treasure the legend keeps is another: a place that was a port of the world. It did not forget the song that made it stop. Whoever races round the island only for photos misses the best.",
    "The whole of Mozambique took its name from this fish that stopped to listen. The lesson is gentle and fits a child on the quay. There are places worth a whole stop, and songs that change the course of a life. If you go to the Island of Mozambique, greet the wooden doors. Do not pull up coral to carry in your pocket. The stone fish stayed because the song deserved it. You can pass, but pass as a guest. The capital moved. The myth stayed, and still answers when someone says the country's name.",
  ],
  "mz:1": [
    "In Gorongosa National Park, in Mozambique, the forest is not only a map of trees. The people say it has a spirit, made of the ancestors and of the master animals. There is the old elephant, who walks as if the land remembered his feet. There is also the lion who is not named at night, so as not to call him needlessly. Before going in, the elders ask for silence. The bush there is someone. You do not go in shouting at everything that moves in the tall grass.",
    "There was a time of great war in the country, and Gorongosa was left almost empty. The animals went away, the paths closed, and the people said the spirits had grown angry. It was not an angry shouting. It was an absence. Where once you heard a branch break under a heavy body, there was wind. Sofala's children grew up hearing that the forest was sad. It was like a house whose people had left without a word. The legend does not hide that time, but guards what was left.",
    "Then peace came, slowly, and the animals began to return. The elephants drew trails again. The lions had shade again. The people said the spirits had accepted the return, because people no longer entered only to take. Gorongosa being born again is a modern miracle, of science and care. The legend gives it a soul for children to understand. It is not enough to tell numbers. You have to tell that the forest forgave enough to open its door again. The jeep can wait.",
    "Those who hunt too much, people say, find the path closed and the river crooked. Those who go in with a guide and with no hunger to own may see the lion's tail far off. It vanishes in the grass, like a short leave. It is not an invitation to come close. It is a sign that the forest still shows a little of itself. The child of Sofala hears this before seeing the park. They learn that the elephant is not a toy. At night, the lion's name stays in a closed mouth.",
    "Mozambique tells the Gorongosa forest as people tell a person who suffered and came back. If you go there one day, do not take only the camera. Take a soft step and the wish to leave without having taken anything but memory. The spirits, in this story, do not ask for fear. They ask that peace last long enough for the animals to stay. The lion without a name at night and the old elephant are the visible masters. The rest is good shadow. Go in as a visitor, and the house may let you see the tail.",
  ],
  "mz:2": [
    "Maize was not born in the villages of Mozambique. It came from far away, from the Americas, in boats and in trades. Folklore adopted it as if it were a relative who arrived and stayed. In the pot it is called by many names. Xima, or sadza, is the centre of the table, the meal that holds the day. Children ask how a grain from outside became so much of home. The grandparents do not speak of maps first. They speak of a bird, of a hunger and of a rule.",
    "In one tale, a girl called Sifa saw a small bird drop yellow grains on the path to the field. Where a grain touched the earth, a straight plant grew. Sifa did not pick everything up for her basket. She followed the bird, step by step, and sowed with it. Soon the whole field threatened to turn golden. At home, her mother made the first mashira, the hot porridge, and called the neighbours. The bird stayed in the edge tree, watching the pot. Would it belong to one house or many?",
    "In another version, there was a year of hunger, with the store empty. Children looked at a pot with no bottom. The ancestors, people say, sent the maize at that time. It was not a prize for one clever farmer, but a gift with a law. The law was short: share. Whoever hid the sack found the next field weak. Whoever opened the door at meal time saw the plant return. Sifa understood the same. The bird came back on the day she took a bowl to the sick neighbour.",
    "The sadza in the centre of the table is a gift with a rule, not a kitchen accident. Mozambique eats this legend every day, even when nobody tells it out loud. The grain has history. It crossed the sea, entered the land and stayed, because the village knew how to receive it. Without sharing, the bird does not return and the yellow of the field fades. With sharing, the next season finds the tall plant again. The spoon passes from hand to hand without shame.",
    "If you sit down to a meal of xima, remember Sifa and the bird. Maize does not ask you to know the whole map. It asks you not to close the pot when there is someone else at the door. Old hunger taught the law, and the law still fits in one extra bowl. Mozambique kept the grain from outside and made it home. A child who understands this understands the country too. What comes from far away can stay, if the table is wide enough.",
  ],
  "mz:3": [
    "In the languages of Mozambique, the clever rabbit has names like Pululu. The lion is still the big one who thinks his voice is enough to give orders. The Makua, the Tsonga and the Sena tell the pair in different ways. The fireside in the Centre and the North, though, knows them both by heart. In this version, they meet by the Zambezi, under a baobab at Tete. It is a hot day when even the water seems lazy. The lion was hungry for importance. The rabbit was hungry to reach the next day.",
    "The lion backed Pululu against the trunk. He said: ‘Today the river is mine, the fish is mine, and you are the dessert.’ Pululu made a comic bow. He answered: ‘Before me, another lion comes, bigger. He lives at the bottom of the water and says you are afraid.’ The king of the bush did not like that. He went to the calm bank and saw his own mane trembling in the mirror. He believed it was an insult with paws. He roared, and the face in the depths roared too.",
    "The lion threw himself at the reflection. The water of the Zambezi closed over his head. It gave him back coughing, wet, with no audience and no lunch. Pululu, up there, did not throw stones. He only said: ‘The other lion was you. The water has no separate king.’ The big one came out through the mud, angry and ashamed. Nobody was hurt except pride. Children laugh at this part, because a wet king is easier to understand than a speech. The grandfather lets the laughter end first.",
    "The grandfather says, without crushing the story, that strength without thought ends at the bank, dripping. He says too that Pululu should not make the trick his only home. One day the Zambezi may not give him a branch. The Makua, the Tsonga and the Sena change the small animal or the waterhole. The message travels the same. It is a cousin of the tales of Angola and Kenya. Still it is Mozambican when the baobab is the one at Tete. The place enters the moral.",
    "If you hear a roar, do not jump straight away. If you are small, do not use cleverness only to humiliate. The fireside wants both things. The wet lion teaches the big one. The rabbit on the branch teaches the care not to turn laughter into cruelty. Mozambique keeps the pair because the river is wide and life is too. In the end, Pululu comes down from the baobab only when the bank is empty. The small one beats the roar. The grandfather leaves the moral in the air.",
  ],
  "mz:4": [
    "In Maputo Bay lives a mermaid, a sister of the kianda of Angola. She appears to the fishermen of Costa do Sol and to the boats going to and from Inhaca. She does not come to comb her hair on a postcard rock. She comes to remind us that the estuary is home. The mangrove, with its roots in the mud, is the nursery of the small fish. Without it, tomorrow's net comes back empty. Legend and science say the same sentence in different clothes. The bay needs cleaning, respect and care.",
    "Sometimes the mermaid sings in Changana, quietly, like someone calling the tide by name. Sometimes she sings nothing and only turns the wind. Then the boat understands that the day is not for going out. The older fishermen let the first small fish go back, not from fear of a monster, but by agreement. The lady of the bay does not ask for parties. She asks that no rubbish is thrown into the estuary. A plastic bag on a mangrove root shuts a door on fish not yet born.",
    "A boy called Hélio, in the busy heart of Maputo, threw an empty bag into the water. The beach already had others. That week the wind turned, and his uncle's nets came back light. His grandmother took Hélio to Costa do Sol and said: ‘The mermaid is not angry with you. She is showing you the estuary.’ The two picked up what the tide had left on the sand, bag by bag, without a speech. The next day the wind was right. Nobody swore they had seen a tail.",
    "Maputo is a big city, with a market, an avenue and hurry, and still this lady stays beside it. The children hear the warning before they learn the word mangrove. The bay is lovely and it is fragile. The legend puts a woman in the water so that care has a face. If the face does not appear, the wind still comes. Hélio began keeping the bag in his pocket until he found a bin. It is a small gesture. The tide is made of small gestures, repeated by many people.",
    "The mermaid of Maputo Bay is the law of the tide in the shape of a woman. She does not frighten you for fun. She reminds us that the fish is born in the roots and that rubbish is not a wave. If you go to Costa do Sol, leave the sand as you would find it. Imagine you were the bay yourself. Whether she sings in Changana or quiets the wind, the message is one. The city can grow. The estuary is not a bin. The home gives back fish, path and an afternoon without shame.",
  ],
  "cv:0": [
    "On the island of Fogo, in Cape Verde, Pico do Fogo is a living volcano. It is tall, dark and sometimes smoking like a forgotten pot. The people say a giant sleeps inside. In another version, a shepherd played the bagpipe so much and so loudly that the land woke the old fire. It never put it fully back to sleep. Both stories serve the same peak. The child of the island sees the smoke and does not only say ‘volcano’. They say the giant's name, a relative who snores on his side.",
    "When the ground shakes and the lava comes down, the elders say the giant has turned over in bed. It was like that in 1995, and again in 2014. The houses of Chã das Caldeiras, the village inside the crater, suffered. The people left with what fitted in their arms. It is a tale to say that the crater is the home of someone bigger. People learn to leave in time and come back when the stone cools. Respect is not eternal flight.",
    "Chã das Caldeiras always comes back. The vines in the ash return, and so does the wine born of that hot ground. The stubbornness of those who call the crater their place returns too. The giant is no cartoon enemy. He is the peak, a difficult relative. He asks for distance on smoky days and gives new earth on calm days. Children help carry water and do not go near the hot mouth to play. Whoever loves the crater does not provoke it, and does not abandon it either.",
    "A grandfather of Chã das Caldeiras planted a new vine in the young ash. It was after the last time the giant turned over. He told his grandson: ‘He is sleeping again. We stay, but we stay watchful.’ The grandson learned to look at the summit before looking at the game. If the smoke changed colour, the family already knew the way out. There was no hero fighting the peak. There was a village treating the fire like an enormous grandfather. He can knock over the table and leave the ground fertile.",
    "Cape Verde tells of Pico do Fogo as a relative, not as a monster on a poster. The legend asks for respect, not an escape that lasts a lifetime. Whoever loves the crater plants again in the ash and keeps the bagpipe for festivals. If you see the peak in the distance, greet it in silence. The wine, the ash and the stubbornness are the island's answer. The giant may turn over. The people of Fogo, stubborn and watchful, put their home back inside the same circle of dark stone.",
  ],
};

export default PART;
